-#### 2D Banded Contouring
-Finds the 2D region where an interval $[L, H]$ of values exists (banded contour). The region is divided into squares aligned with the grid [S3, unofficial]. For each square, corners are assigned 0 (value $< L$), 1 ($L \le value \le H$), or 2 (value $> H$). This results in $3^4 = 81$ cases, reduced to 13 canonical cases via symmetry [S3, unofficial]. Case numbers in the first row are 0, 40, 1, 2, 3; second row 5, 8, 10, 11, 13; last row 14, 16, 20 [S3, unofficial].
+Generates filled contour bands between an upper and lower threshold. It classifies cells into three states (below, within, above) rather than two, allowing for continuous filled regions.
+
+### 2D Banded Contouring
+Finds the 2D region where an interval $[L, H]$ of values exists. Each square corner is assigned a ternary value: 0 if value $< L$, 1 if $L \le value \le H$, 2 if value $> H$ [S3, unofficial]. This results in $3^4 = 81$ initial cases, reduced to 13 canonical cases after accounting for symmetries (rotational, mirror, high/low) [S3, unofficial]. The case number is determined by the base-3 number formed by the corner states [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$, as each cell is processed independently [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$ for the input grid; output size depends on contour density.
-*   **Parallelism:** Embarrassingly parallel; all cells are independent [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; efficient for 2D data; forms the basis for 3D algorithms [S2, S11].
-*   **Limitations:** Produces jagged contours at low resolution; ambiguous saddle points require extra logic; not suitable for 3D surfaces directly [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When to Use:** 2D contour maps, weather maps, slice-based 3D visualization [S2][S5, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** 3D isosurface extraction (use Marching Cubes); high-precision smooth surfaces (use Dual Contouring or subdivision) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$ for the input grid and output geometry [S2].
+*   **Parallelism:** The algorithm is embarrassingly parallel because all cells are processed independently [S2, S11].
+*   **Performance:** A naive implementation performs linear interpolation twice per edge for isolines, resulting in duplicate vertices [S2, S11]. Caching interpolation results for one row of the input grid can reduce computational overhead [S2]. Using indexed geometric primitives reduces output size [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, robust for 2D data, fast [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Produces jagged contours at low resolutions; aliasing if not interpolated correctly; ambiguous saddle points in 3D (not 2D squares) or non-planar 2D embeddings [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## Background
-Marching Squares is the 2D analog of the Marching Cubes algorithm [S2, S11]. It was developed as a method for generating contour lines from scalar fields, commonly used in topographic mapping and scientific visualization [S2]. The algorithm processes each cell independently, determining the topology of the isosurface intersection based on the scalar values at the cell's vertices relative to an isolevel [S2]. It is widely used in tools like VTK and ParaView for 2D isocontour extraction [S5, unofficial].
+Marching Squares is the 2D analog of the Marching Cubes algorithm [S2, S11]. It was developed as a method to generate contours for two-dimensional scalar fields, such as topographic maps or weather isobars [S2]. The algorithm processes each cell in the grid independently, calculating a cell index using comparisons of the contour level with the data values at the cell corners, and using a pre-built lookup table to describe the output geometry [S2]. It is used in scientific data visualization, such as the 'Hurricane Isabel Scientific Data Visualization' project [S5, unofficial].
 
